QUESTIONS TO BE ANSWERED - Scenario-based questions 
(You may look at the Handbook, but NOT use AI) 
1. A manager tells an employee that she will receive a promotion only if she agrees 
to go on a date with him. Does this qualify as sexual harassment? Why? What if 
the manager is a female employee? 

There is no gender bias to qualify for sexual harassment compalint

2. An employee receives jokes that have a sexual overtone in the office WhatsApp 
group. The group has both male and female employees. Some ignore it, some 
enjoy it and one woman is offended by it. Can this fall within the scope of 
workplace sexual harassment? Explain. 

Yes, it could be Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013 (POSH Act)—this conduct qualifies as harassment because it creates a hostile and offensive work environment.

3. An employee wants to file a complaint but is physically unable to do so. Who 
may file the complaint on her behalf, and what additional requirement must be 
met? 

Some authorized individuals may file it on her behalf.
• Relative or friend
• Co-worker

4. What is the purpose of PoSH Training? 

